<% @title = "Flora" %>

<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
  <h1 class="text-8xl font-bold text-rose-500">Flora</h1>

  <div class="mt-12 space-y-16">

    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-8 items-start">
      <img
        src="<%= image_url("laurus-nobilis.jpg") %>"
        alt="Laurus nobilis"
        data-attribution="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laurus_nobilis_g1.jpg"
        class="rounded-2xl w-full sm:w-48 sm:h-48 h-56 object-cover flex-shrink-0"
      />
      <div>
        <p class="text-2xl font-bold">Bay Laurel</p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 italic text-base">Laurus nobilis</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-rose-500 uppercase tracking-wide">Best pruning time</p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 text-base">Late spring to early summer, after new growth has hardened off.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-rose-500 uppercase tracking-wide">Fun fact</p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 text-base">The ancient Greeks and Romans wove bay laurel into wreaths to crown victorious athletes, generals, and poets — the phrase "resting on your laurels" comes directly from this tradition.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-8 items-start">
      <img
        src="<%= image_url("glossy-abelia.jpg") %>"
        alt="Abelia x grandiflora"
        data-attribution="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abelia-grandiflora-total.JPG"
        class="rounded-2xl w-full sm:w-48 sm:h-48 h-56 object-cover flex-shrink-0"
      />
      <div>
        <p class="text-2xl font-bold">Glossy Abelia</p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 italic text-base">Abelia &times; grandiflora</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-rose-500 uppercase tracking-wide">Best pruning time</p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 text-base">Late winter to early spring, before new growth begins. Light shaping is fine throughout the growing season.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-rose-500 uppercase tracking-wide">Fun fact</p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 text-base">Glossy Abelia is a hybrid that doesn't exist anywhere in the wild — it was bred around 1886 by crossing two Chinese species, <em>Abelia chinensis</em> and <em>Abelia uniflora</em>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-8 items-start">
      <img
        src="<%= image_url("juniper-gold-lace.jpg") %>"
        alt="Juniperus chinensis Gold Lace"
        data-attribution="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juniperus_chinensis_cult._aurea.JPG"
        class="rounded-2xl w-full sm:w-48 sm:h-48 h-56 object-cover flex-shrink-0"
      />
      <div>
        <p class="text-2xl font-bold">Juniper Gold Lace</p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 italic text-base">Juniperus chinensis &lsquo;Gold Lace&rsquo;</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-rose-500 uppercase tracking-wide">Best pruning time</p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 text-base">Early spring before new growth emerges. Selectively trim tips to shape — avoid cutting into old wood, which won't regenerate.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-rose-500 uppercase tracking-wide">Fun fact</p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 text-base">Gold Lace keeps its golden-yellow color year-round rather than fading in summer heat — a trait that made it a standout among landscape architects looking for reliable color without flowers.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-8 items-start">
      <img
        src="<%= image_url("crepe-myrtle.jpg") %>"
        alt="Lagerstroemia indica"
        data-attribution="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%28MHNT%29_Lagerstroemia_indica_-_inflorescences.jpg"
        class="rounded-2xl w-full sm:w-48 sm:h-48 h-56 object-cover flex-shrink-0"
      />
      <div>
        <p class="text-2xl font-bold">Crepe Myrtle</p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 italic text-base">Lagerstroemia indica</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-rose-500 uppercase tracking-wide">Best pruning time</p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 text-base">Late winter to early spring, before buds break. The less you cut, the better.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-rose-500 uppercase tracking-wide">Fun fact</p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 text-base">Horticulturists coined the term &ldquo;crape murder&rdquo; to describe the widespread Southern practice of severely topping crepe myrtles each year — a habit that weakens the tree and leaves stubby, ugly knobs called &ldquo;crape knuckles.&rdquo;</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-8 items-start">
      <img
        src="<%= image_url("chinese-holly.jpg") %>"
        alt="Ilex cornuta"
        data-attribution="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ilex_cornuta_RF.jpg"
        class="rounded-2xl w-full sm:w-48 sm:h-48 h-56 object-cover flex-shrink-0"
      />
      <div>
        <p class="text-2xl font-bold">Chinese Holly</p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 italic text-base">Ilex cornuta</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-rose-500 uppercase tracking-wide">Best pruning time</p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 text-base">Late winter to early spring, before new growth begins. Prune after berry display has ended.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-rose-500 uppercase tracking-wide">Fun fact</p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 text-base">Unlike most hollies, Chinese Holly can produce berries without pollination from a male plant — a phenomenon called parthenocarpy. Its vivid red berries are toxic to humans but a critical winter food source for songbirds.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-8 items-start">
      <img
        src="<%= image_url("water-oak.jpg") %>"
        alt="Quercus nigra"
        data-attribution="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quercus_nigra_USDA.jpg"
        class="rounded-2xl w-full sm:w-48 sm:h-48 h-56 object-cover flex-shrink-0"
      />
      <div>
        <p class="text-2xl font-bold">Water Oak</p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 italic text-base">Quercus nigra</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-rose-500 uppercase tracking-wide">Best pruning time</p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 text-base">Late winter to early spring while the tree is dormant, or in midsummer after the initial flush of growth has hardened off.</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-semibold text-rose-500 uppercase tracking-wide">Fun fact</p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 text-base">Water oak leaves are famously inconsistent — on the same tree you can find leaves shaped like spatulas, duck feet, and three-lobed fans, making it one of the most variable-leaved oaks in North America.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</div>
